# ===========================================
# DigiLog API .env  (apps/api/.env)
# Copy to .env and fill in the secrets marked CHANGE_ME.
# Loaded by `import 'dotenv/config'` at apps/api/src/app.ts:1.
# ===========================================
#
# Post Phase-7 (2026-06-17): the old data-ingestion + TimescaleDB layer was
# removed — no TSDB_* or UNS_* keys exist anymore. MQTT came BACK on 2026-08-06
# with the Instruments ingest bridge, but it is a different thing entirely: an
# external EMQX broker feeding `POST`-equivalent readings into the same ingest
# core the HTTP route uses. See the instrument-ingest block below.

# ─── PostgreSQL (Prisma) ──────────────────────────────────
# Also used by graphile-worker — it shares the same DB.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://digilog:CHANGE_ME@localhost:5432/digilog_db?schema=public

# ─── JWT Configuration ───────────────────────────────────
# Generate the secret: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(64).toString('base64'))"
# REQUIRED in production; the API exits fatally if it is missing or <32 chars.
# (VERIFICATION_TOKEN_SECRET used to sit beside it. It signed the token
# POST /api/auth/verify issued, which nothing consumed; route, helpers and
# secret were removed — audit 2026-10-03, SEC-5. A leftover value in an
# existing .env is read by nothing and can be deleted.)
JWT_SECRET=change-this-to-a-secure-random-string
# ─── Audit chain integrity (21 CFR §11.10(e)) ────────────────────────────────
# These two are a PAIR. Setting only the first leaves the chain forgeable.
#
# AUDIT_CHAIN_KEY — when set, new audit rows use keyed HMAC-SHA256
#   (checksum_version=3) so an actor with DB write access but no key cannot
#   forge the chain. Unset = unkeyed SHA-256, and because the checksum lives in
#   the same table it protects, a DB-level actor can recompute a fully
#   self-consistent forged chain that passes verify-chain.
#   MUST be stable + backed up OUTSIDE the database — losing or changing it
#   makes every v3 row permanently unverifiable.
#
# AUDIT_CHAIN_KEYED_FROM — the chain_position at which keying began. Every row
#   at/after it MUST be v3; verify-chain reports KEYED_ERA_DOWNGRADE otherwise.
#   Without this anchor a v3 row can be relabelled checksum_version=NULL,
#   recomputed with the unkeyed formula (no key needed), the forward chain
#   relinked, and verification still PASSES. Set it to 0 on a fresh install;
#   on an existing install set it to the CURRENT head position captured with
#   the API stopped (verify-chain reports highestPosition).
#
# Ordering matters. Stop API → read head → write BOTH vars → start API. Rows
# written between capturing the head and the key loading would land after the
# boundary as unkeyed, and verification would then fail permanently on a chain
# that was never tampered with.
#
# AUDIT_CHAIN_KEY=change-this-to-a-secure-random-string
# AUDIT_CHAIN_KEYED_FROM=0

# AUDIT_HARD_DELETE_ENABLED — whether audit records can be PHYSICALLY DELETED.
#
#   SHIPPED OFF (audit 2026-10-03, DATA-8). Every installer template
#   (scripts/install.ps1, scripts/linux/digilog-lib.sh) and this example write
#   `false`, so a fresh install cannot destroy §11 history until someone
#   deliberately changes this line. The CODE default is unchanged: with the
#   variable UNSET the capability is ON (lib/audit-immutability.ts), and only
#   the exact string "false" turns it off, so a typo cannot silently remove a
#   capability an operator believes they still have. An existing .env without
#   the line therefore keeps hard delete enabled — add it.
#
#   When enabled, DELETE /api/audit/:id and POST /api/audit/bulk-delete can
#   permanently remove §11 records. They are already gated on the AUDIT_DELETE
#   permission, re-authenticated, rate-limited to 5/min, and they write a
#   meta-audit row naming the deletion first — but they DO break the
#   tamper-evident hash chain, and verify-chain will report the downstream
#   chain invalid from that position onward. With the switch off they answer
#   403 AUDIT_HARD_DELETE_DISABLED and point at redact.
#
#   21 CFR Part 11 §11.10(e) calls for audit trails that cannot be modified or
#   obscured. POST /api/audit/bulk-redact removes record CONTENT without
#   breaking the chain and covers the legitimate "this must not be retained"
#   case. Set this to true only on an installation whose validation package
#   records why physical deletion is permitted.
AUDIT_HARD_DELETE_ENABLED=false
#
#   KNOWN GAP while this is false: the Audit Trail page gates its delete
#   affordance on the AUDIT_DELETE PERMISSION (`can('audit.delete')` in
#   apps/web/src/routes/audit/index.tsx), not on this flag — so a user who
#   holds that permission still sees the button and gets a 403 on click.
#   Backend enforcement is correct and complete; only the affordance is stale.
#   Closing it properly means the SERVER telling the client the capability is
#   off (the pattern used by /api/instruments/attribute-access/me), never the
#   client re-deriving it — see the "Never reconstruct server-side decision
#   logic in the client" rule in the root CLAUDE.md. Until then, prefer
#   revoking AUDIT_DELETE from every role as well, which removes both the
#   button and the permission.

# INSTRUMENT_TOKEN_KEY — encrypts the reversible copy of each device access
#   token (AES-256-GCM), so an operator can be SHOWN the token a device is using
#   instead of regenerating it and going out to reconfigure the device. This
#   reverses spec decision D5; see §3.1 of
#   docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-04-instruments-inbound-ingestion-design.md
#   and apps/api/src/modules/instruments/token-crypto.ts.
#
#   Generate:
#     node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#   32 bytes, base64 or hex. Anything else is REFUSED, never truncated.
#
#   OPTIONAL. Unset means tokens are simply not viewable — device
#   authentication and telemetry ingest read the SHA-256 hash and never this
#   key, so they work identically with or without it.
#
#   ROTATING IT ORPHANS EVERY EXISTING CIPHERTEXT. Tokens issued under the old
#   key then report TOKEN_DECRYPT_FAILED on reveal (the devices keep working —
#   only the reveal breaks) and must be regenerated to become viewable again.
#   Back this value up with the same care as AUDIT_CHAIN_KEY.
# INSTRUMENT_TOKEN_KEY=
# Audit 2026-05-29 S-8: default JWT TTL reduced from 8h → 1h.
# Override via JWT_EXPIRY_HOURS (integer hours, clamped at signToken).
JWT_EXPIRES_IN=1h

# ─── Server Configuration ────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=production
API_PORT=3000
# Vite dev-server port, read by scripts/start-app.ps1. Pinned rather than
# auto-selected — if this port is busy the launcher stops instead of drifting to
# the next free one. Set a distinct value per checkout on a multi-checkout machine.
WEB_PORT=5175
# Run the API over TLS using certs/server.key + certs/server.crt.
# REQUIRED so the Android APK (built with VITE_API_URL=https://...) can connect.
API_HTTPS=true
# Set to 1 (or a hop-count) only when behind a known reverse proxy.
# Default false → req.ip uses the raw socket address (un-spoofable).
# TRUST_PROXY=1
# The public origin of this server as the OAuth2 mail provider (Azure AD /
# Google) knows it — scheme + host[:port], no path, no trailing slash. It is the
# base of the redirect URI shown under Notification Settings and sent on every
# /authorize and token exchange (audit 2026-10-03, SEC-6). Set it whenever the
# address users type differs from what the API sees on the socket (a reverse
# proxy, a DNS name in front of an IP). Unset = derived from each request via
# Fastify's req.protocol / req.host, which honour X-Forwarded-* ONLY when
# TRUST_PROXY is on; the raw forwarded headers are never read directly.
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://digilog.pharma.local

# ─── Instrument ingest: raw TCP listener ─────────────────
# Port for the raw-TCP telemetry listener (instruments that speak neither HTTP
# nor MQTT). LEAVE THIS UNSET unless you actually have such a device — unset
# means the listener does not start at all, so upgrading never silently opens a
# port on an operator's machine.
#
# RAW TCP IS PLAINTEXT. There is no TLS on this listener, so the device token
# crosses the wire in the clear and anyone who can see the traffic can replay
# it. Keep this port on a trusted VLAN, never expose it to the internet, and
# prefer HTTPS / WSS / MQTTS for anything that leaves the plant floor. This is
# inherent to the transport, not a gap waiting to be closed.
#
# Protocol: line-delimited JSON over a plain socket. The FIRST line must be
#   {"token":"<device token>"}
# and every line after it is one reading, e.g. {"temp":21.5} — or, from a
# gateway, {"devices":{"CHILD-CODE":{"temp":21.5}}}. Tokens are the same device
# tokens the HTTP ingest route accepts; a token works on any listener.
#
# Built-in limits (not configurable by env, deliberately): 64 KB per line
# — the same cap as the HTTP body limit, and what keeps a gateway batch under
# Postgres's bind-parameter ceiling — a 5s deadline to send the token line,
# a 15-minute post-auth idle timeout, and 200 concurrent connections.
# INGEST_TCP_PORT=1884

# URL of the EXTERNAL MQTT broker (EMQX) the ingest bridge subscribes to. LEAVE
# UNSET unless a broker is actually running — unset means no outbound connection
# is attempted at all. Devices publish to `instruments/<code>/telemetry`; the
# bridge subscribes `instruments/+/telemetry` at QoS 1.
#
# THE BROKER MUST BE CONFIGURED, or this transport is insecure. Two settings,
# both required (full walkthrough in LOCAL_SETUP_WINDOWS.md):
#   1. An `http` authenticator pointed at POST /api/ingest/v1/mqtt-auth. Devices
#      present the device token as the MQTT USERNAME and the instrument code as
#      the MQTT CLIENT ID; the hook denies any pair that does not match.
#   2. `authorization { no_match = deny }` plus an acl.conf rule permitting
#      publish only to `instruments/${clientid}/telemetry`. WITHOUT THIS, any
#      device with a valid token can publish any other instrument's telemetry —
#      the bridge cannot detect it, because a subscriber never learns who
#      published.
#
# Built-in limits (not configurable by env, deliberately): 64 KB per message
# (the same cap as the HTTP body limit) and 500 queued messages.
#
# MQTTS (devices over TLS on 8883) needs NOTHING here. It is a broker-side
# listener; the API is unaffected. See LOCAL_SETUP_WINDOWS.md §9B.
#
# THIS URL STAYS PLAINTEXT ON PURPOSE, and it is a different call from the other
# two hops. Three hops carry three different secrets:
#   device -> broker    : the device token   -> TLS on 8883 (mqtts)
#   broker -> API hook  : the device token   -> HTTPS + verify_peer
#   API bridge -> broker: the BRIDGE's own static credential, on loopback only
#                         -> plaintext, deliberately
# Nothing on the third hop is a device secret and it never leaves 127.0.0.1;
# anyone able to read it already owns the host and the TLS key with it.
#
# CHANGE THIS THE MOMENT THE BROKER IS NOT ON LOOPBACK. A remote broker turns
# the bridge credential into a real wire secret. It is not a one-line edit:
# app.ts forwards only username/password to mqtt.connect(), so there is no CA
# option, and Node does not read the Windows trust store — an mqtts:// bridge
# needs new code and a new env var, not just a scheme change.
# MQTT_BROKER_URL=mqtt://127.0.0.1:1883
# The BRIDGE's own broker credentials — not a device token. Since 2026-09-08 the
# HTTP auth hook itself admits this username/password pair (constant-time
# compare), so ONE authenticator is enough — this is what the Setup.exe installer
# configures. A built-in-database user ahead of the hook still works (the dev
# box has one). Set BOTH or the hook denies the bridge name outright.
# MQTT_USERNAME=digilog-bridge
# MQTT_PASSWORD=
# The bridge's MQTT client id. Defaults to "digilog-ingest-bridge" and must stay
# STABLE across restarts — the durable (clean=false) session that holds messages
# published while the API was down is keyed on it. Only set this if you ever run
# more than one API instance against one broker; two clients sharing an id kick
# each other off in a loop.
# MQTT_CLIENT_ID=digilog-ingest-bridge

# ─── CORS Configuration ──────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated list of allowed origins.
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5175
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5175,http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:3000

# ─── Upload Configuration ────────────────────────────────
UPLOAD_DIR=./uploads
MAX_FILE_SIZE=5242880

# ─── PDF rendering (Phase 3 of windows-friendly-rewrite) ──
# puppeteer-core + Edge auto-detected via detectEdgePath(). Set this only
# to override the auto-detected browser binary.
# PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH=C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft\Edge\Application\msedge.exe
